This paper investigates the normalized ground state solution for the given biharmonic Choquard type equation \begin{align*}
  \begin{split}
  \left\{
  \begin{array}{ll}
  \Delta^2u-\beta\Delta u=\lambda u+\left(\frac{1}{|x|^\mu}*F(u)\right)f(u), & x\in\mathbb{R}^4, \\
  \displaystyle\int_{\mathbb{R}^4}u^2dx=c^2, &
  \end{array}
  \right.
  \end{split}
  \end{align*} under specific conditions where $0<\mu<2$, $\beta\geq0$, $0<\lambda<\infty$, and the function $f$ exhibits exponential critical growth with respect to the Trudinger-Moser inequality. By employing a Lagrange multiplier technique in conjunction with the mountain pass theorem, we establish the existence of a normalized ground state solution for the described equation.

Recent precision data from $B$ meson decays have enabled the determination of lepton flavor specific couplings, providing highly precise constraints on new physics models. Leveraging these data sets, our group has developed a new amplitude for the decay $\Lambda_b 	o p 	au  ar{
u}$ within the $U_1$ leptoquark model. This new amplitude complements the existing $\mathcal{O}(\alpha_s)$ calculations and the lepton flavor specific $\mathcal{O}(\alpha)$ loop calculation. By combining $ eta$-conformal heavy quark effective theory form factors with these new amplitude components, we explore the impact of $b 	o c$ transitions on the branching ratio and differential decay rate of $\Lambda_b 	o p  ar{
u}$. Our analysis reveals that $b 	o c$ measurements significantly influence the predicted outcomes for $\Lambda_b 	o p 	au  ar{
u}$ decay, with variations in the heavy quark effective theory amplitude affecting the predicted outcomes by up to a factor of two.
